 of their country themselves so  is it only the state states that are  building mosques there are there any  private mosques being built there are  private mosques I mean you know building  a mosque is a benevolent act so if you  have the means you might want to  consider building a mosque or  patronising one but what I'm really  interested is in the way in which these  four states because what I argue in the  book is that these four states represent  what might be the vision for the Muslim  world today so Iran Turkey Saudi Arabia  and the United Arab Emirates have a  vision for what they think of the Muslim  community now today and they're using  these state-sponsored mosques as a way  of propagating their ideology  disseminating their thoughts for what  this Muslim community might be and so  that's my focus primarily I'm not really  looking at every type of I was but this  very particular typology and what led  you to this project I was actually  nominated for a Carnegie Award a few  years ago by a colleague at UCLA Lynn  hunt and the mandate of that cycle of  award was to understand the Muslim world  today or the modern Muslim world as you  mentioned my work has also been and  primarily has been on the early modern  period and so what I wanted to think of  was what is the role of history in  modern Islam so that was my starting  point and I wanted to look at this form  of architecture which again as I  mentioned is ubiquitous but since at  least 30 years  modern mosques in the Middle East or in  fact you could say all over the Islamic  world are very historical that they make  these very powerful historical  references for example mosques built by  the Turkish Republic our Ottoman inform  right so they are going back to the  classical 16th century  to define a particular type of  architecture and I was very curious to  understand why why is it that they are  going so far deep into the past and what  does history mean in this context  so what is at stake and now t