econstructions in Syria in Iraq in  Lebanon so each of these countries has a  very different way of going about this  sponsorship and that's what I was really  curious to find out more okay and let's  define the term transnational versus a  traditional mosque you mentioned them  building mosques and other countries is  that smart a transnational term exactly  exactly and and and I think what I  wanted to do was to try and find a way  of distinguishing this idea of say the  global right because that's a term that  has also been used when we're talking  about the contemporary period there are  other ways of thinking about same  abilities is another way in which people  have talked about community is going  beyond national borders and certainly  media and so on allow us to do that but  what I argue in the book and why I  really am committed to the term  transnational is that I think the nation  is still at the center of political  discourse particularly in the  contemporary Middle East that is to say  that the types of decision making public  policy the way in which public's are  created themselves are really from the  point of view of the state and the  nation and so whether the buildings are  within the sovereign boundaries of a  country such as Iran or Turkey or the  United Arab Emirates or outside I argue  that it's still the national that is  playing into these decisions making and  how is that decision-making reflected in  the actual building of the mosque I mean  I speak to specific countries yeah yeah  yeah no certainly so the choices  for example where these mosques will be  barracks right so for example the Saudi  government in the 1980s sponsored of  what is now understood to be the  national mosque in Pakistan in Islamabad  which is known as the King Faisal mosque  it was completed in 1986 and it's named  after King Faisal who is the ruler of  Saudi Arabia before his death and this  mosque is given as a gift in a sense to  the people of Pakistan now as you 