e close to some other kind of  commercial venture that helped  supplement its own income and that would  have been an endowment that was part of  the mosque well what the coach at EBI  has done is taken this modern form right  so of the supermarket of the parking  garage but indeed typologically it's not  that distinct but what it has done is  it's responding to its time right right  so it's got this incredible populism  consumerism to it and so that projects  an idea of what the Turkish government  sees as its pillars of strength now  right yeah and I would imagine really  draws people to the mosque for a variety  of reason from Quran classes for  children for adults learning Arabic a  whole slew of activities take place  there now in contrast and I think this  is also interesting to note is that the  United Arab Emirates for example has its  national mosques for example desired  more  in a booth hobby which is monumental  it's incredibly large and beautiful they  have not chosen one style because the  the idea was that she excited wanted to  build a country in the nation that  brought people from all over the world  and so that mosque makes references to  many different historical periods many  different countries they've brought in  artisans from India from North Africa  the design of the mosaics is by this  British artist so they brought the world  to themselves and that's part of the way  in which the United Arab Emirates wants  to project its vision of Islam second  these mosques the state mosques in the  United Arab Emirates are also community  centers but also cultural centers so  they are open to non-muslims and they  see as part of their mandate to be open  to a very wide community to teach them  about Islam and to project a vision of a  tolerant progressive Islam so that is  very different from what the Turkish  case is but it tells us a lot about how  the United Arab Emirates sees itself  today and moving forward so the mosque  is part of this the idea i